INT. GRAND CENTRAL WAITING HALL - NIGHT

1946. High arched windows, benches of dark oak. A great board of arrivals flicks over names. Steam from distant trains. A few soldiers asleep on their duffels.

IMELDA CASTELLANOS, 40s, in a worn wool coat, sits on a bench with her hands in her lap. A suitcase at her feet. A man's hat on top of it.

WINIFRED BOYLE, 60s, in tweed, with a prim hat and a leather handbag, perches at the far end of the bench. She has been watching the board for hours.

ARTHUR PEMBROKE, 30s, in a demobbed uniform with the insignia ripped from the sleeve, walks the aisle. Stops. Stares at Imelda.

ARTHUR
Imelda?

She stands. The hat falls from the suitcase. Arthur bends to pick it up, but Winifred gets there first. She holds it out to Imelda with a trembling hand, and Imelda takes it without taking her eyes off him.

IMELDA
Arthur. You are late.

ARTHUR
The trains. I was told.

IMELDA
You were told four years ago.

ARTHUR
I am here now.

Winifred rises, smooths her coat, and walks away down the hall. She does not look back. Arthur steps closer. Imelda lifts the hat between them.

IMELDA
Your father's.

ARTHUR
I thought it was lost.

IMELDA
Nothing is lost, Arthur. Only misplaced.

He puts it on his head. It fits. She straightens it with two fingers.
